 ## Overview
-Triangle Projection Voxelization renders input mesh triangles directly into a 3D voxel grid using a union-based accumulation strategy, where overlapping projections are resolved by retaining the maximum coverage or depth value [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [3-axis rasterization voxelization](2480-3-axis-rasterization-voxelization.md) by treating the voxel grid as a volumetric render target rather than a set of discrete 2D slices, allowing for a single-pass (or few-pass) projection that inherently handles complex geometry intersections without explicit geometric clipping [S1, unofficial].
+Triangle Projection Voxelization renders input mesh triangles directly into a 3D voxel grid using a union-based accumulation strategy, where overlapping projections are resolved by retaining the maximum coverage or depth value [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [3-axis rasterization voxelization](2480-3-axis-rasterization-voxelization.md) by projecting triangles directly into a 3D render target using a fixed three-pass instance draw, relying on the rasterizer's depth/coverage tests to resolve overlaps without explicit geometric clipping [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A set of mesh triangles (indices and vertices), a voxel grid resolution ($N_x, N_y, N_z$), and a camera view (Model-View-Projection matrix).
-*   **Output:** A 3D voxel grid (UAV) where each voxel stores occupancy or depth information derived from the projected triangles.
+*   **Input:** Mesh triangle indices and vertices, a voxel grid resolution, and a camera projection matrix [G].
+*   **Output:** A 3D voxel grid stored as an Unordered Access View (UAV), where each voxel stores occupancy or depth information derived from projected triangles [S1, unofficial].
 
